INT. NIGHT TRAIN "ALBATROSS" - SLEEPER CAR - NIGHT

A narrow corridor of polished mahogany and brass fixtures. Curtains drawn over the windows. Beyond them, the dark rushes by. The carriage sways in a long steady pulse.

INSPECTOR ABEL STROM, 50s, a hat too small for his head, a ticket punch in one hand, walks the corridor slowly, checking doors, listening. At each cabin he pauses. At the third he knocks.

A door opens a crack. MADAME VALENTINE ROUX, 60s, a silk robe and a cold cream mask, peers out with an eye like a coin.

ROUX
Yes?

STROM
Madame. A disturbance reported. Did you hear anything?

ROUX
I hear the train. I hear my own pulse. I hear a gentleman in the corridor who will not let a woman sleep.

STROM
My apologies. Have you seen anyone pass?

She considers him. Behind her, a light flicks on in the next compartment.

ROUX
Only the doctor.

STROM
Which doctor?

ROUX
The one in four.

She shuts the door. He moves on. Cabin four. He raises his hand to knock, hesitates, listens. A soft murmur inside, a woman's voice. He taps.

The door opens. DR. HESTER PRYCE, 40s, a black medical bag in one hand and a wet handkerchief in the other, regards him with sleepless composure.

PRYCE
Inspector.

STROM
Doctor. I'm told you were attending someone.

PRYCE
A porter. A faint. Nothing serious.

STROM
Which porter?

PRYCE
The young one. Giles.

STROM
Ah.

He peers past her shoulder. The compartment is dim. A figure lies on the lower berth, motionless under a blanket.

PORTER GILES, 20s, in a half-buttoned uniform, one hand pressed to his temple, lifts his head and gives a weak, apologetic smile.

GILES
Sorry for the fuss, sir.

STROM
Nothing to apologize for, my boy. Rest.

He steps back. Pryce eases the door half closed. For a moment their eyes hold. She is calm. He is polite. Neither blinks.

STROM (CONT'D)
Doctor, a curious thing. The ticket in your name was bought in Vienna.

PRYCE
It was.

STROM
And yet the conductor reports you boarded in Prague.

PRYCE
He's mistaken.

STROM
He rarely is.

She smiles. It is a small, tired smile.

PRYCE
Inspector, would you care for tea?

STROM
I would like the truth, Doctor.

PRYCE
Over tea.

He thinks. Nods once. She opens the door wider. He enters. The compartment smells of lavender and iodine.

On the lower berth, Giles has gone still. His eyes are open. He is not looking at the Inspector. He is looking at Pryce.

STROM
(gently)
Doctor. Your patient is not breathing.

Pryce does not turn.

PRYCE
I know.

She closes the door. The latch clicks. In the corridor, Madame Roux's door opens again, an inch, and a single coin-bright eye peers out. It watches the door of cabin four for a long time. Then, quietly, it shuts.
